{%- comment -%}
  hp-reviews — the one review section for the whole site.

  Deliberately a thin, branded shell around Judge.me's own app blocks rather
  than markup that prints reviews itself. Two reasons:

  1. Every review shown anywhere on the site then comes from Judge.me. There is
     no second, hand-written source of testimonials to drift out of date or to
     contradict the real ratings — which is what the old hardcoded slider did.
  2. Which Judge.me widget belongs on a page differs (product review widget on
     a product, featured carousel on the homepage, all-reviews elsewhere), and
     an @app block lets the right one be dropped in from the theme editor
     without the theme hardcoding an app block handle it cannot verify.

  Styling lives in assets/hp-reviews.css, shared with the product page, so the
  two surfaces cannot diverge.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign has_title = false
  if section.settings.heading != blank
    assign has_title = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  id: only on the product-scoped section, which is the one a review card
  elsewhere on the site links into. Store-wide sections do not carry it —
  two elements with the same id on one page is what would break the jump.
  Kept distinct from main-product's own #hp-reviews block for the same reason.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div
  {% if section.settings.source != 'store' %}id="product-reviews"{% endif %}
  class="hp-revs hp-revs--{{ section.settings.bg }} hp-revs--{{ section.settings.card_style }}{% if has_title %} hp-revs--has-title{% endif %}"
  style="padding-top: {{ section.settings.padding_top }}px; padding-bottom: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom }}px;"
>
  <div class="hp-revs__inner hp-container">
    {%- if section.settings.eyebrow != blank or has_title or section.settings.text != blank -%}
      <div class="hp-revs__head{% if section.settings.align == 'center' %} hp-revs__head--center{% endif %}">
        {%- if section.settings.eyebrow != blank -%}
          <span class="hp-revs__eyebrow">{{ section.settings.eyebrow }}</span>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if has_title -%}
          <h2 class="hp-revs__title">{{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if section.settings.text != blank -%}
          <p class="hp-revs__text">{{ section.settings.text }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="hp-revs__widget">
      {%- comment -%}
        Judge.me's own app block is the only thing here that is a live widget.
        It loads the app's script, so reviews expand, photos open, sorting and
        paging work and every review is reachable. Whichever block the merchant
        adds decides the scope: "Review widget" on a product page shows that
        product's reviews, "All reviews" anywhere else shows the whole shop's.

        This is rendered for BOTH sources now. The metafield path below is a
        static server-rendered dump with no script behind it — it can show
        reviews but none of them do anything — so it is only a fallback for a
        section that has no block yet, never the preferred path.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign hp_app_block_count = 0 -%}
      {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
        {%- if block.type == '@app' -%}
          {% render block %}
          {%- assign hp_app_block_count = hp_app_block_count | plus: 1 -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}

      {%- if hp_app_block_count == 0 and section.settings.source == 'store' -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          FALLBACK ONLY — reached when this section has no Judge.me block.

          The app writes every published review to shop.metafields.judgeme as
          server-rendered HTML. It needs no app block and it paints on the first
          request, but it is inert: the widget script never mounts on it, so a
          review cannot be expanded, a photo cannot be opened, and there is no
          sorting or paging. Add Judge.me's "All reviews" block to the section
          and that block is used instead, with all of it working.

          It is the classic .jdgm-rev markup rather than the revamped widget's,
          which is why hp-reviews.css carries styling for both.

          A single metafield has a size cap, so once a shop has enough reviews
          Judge.me splits the HTML across several: all_reviews_0, all_reviews_1,
          and so on. Reading only _0 drops every review past the first chunk —
          which is where the newer reviews, including most of the ones with
          photos and video, actually live.

          The keys are looked up BY EXACT NAME, one index at a time. The
          namespace also holds all_reviews_count, all_reviews_rating and a
          JSON-LD Organization blob, all of which "contains all_reviews" would
          match — concatenating those into the markup printed "406 {...} 4.89"
          as visible text above the cards and broke the card markup itself.
          Stop at the first missing index: the chunks are contiguous, so a gap
          means there are no more.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign store_reviews = '' -%}
        {%- for i in (0..19) -%}
          {%- assign chunk_key = 'all_reviews_' | append: i -%}
          {%- assign chunk = shop.metafields.judgeme[chunk_key] -%}
          {%- if chunk == blank -%}
            {%- break -%}
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- assign store_reviews = store_reviews | append: chunk -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
        {%- if store_reviews != blank -%}
          {%- if request.design_mode -%}
            <p class="hp-revs__text">
              These reviews come from Judge.me’s stored copy, so they are shown
              but not interactive — they cannot be expanded, photos will not
              open, and there is no sorting or paging. Add Judge.me’s “All
              reviews” block to this section to get the real widget.
            </p>
          {%- endif -%}
          <div class="jdgm-review-list hp-revs__store" data-hp-store-reviews>
            {{ store_reviews }}
          </div>
        {%- elsif request.design_mode -%}
          <p class="hp-revs__text">
            Add Judge.me’s “All reviews” block to this section to show the whole
            shop’s reviews.
          </p>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if hp_app_block_count == 0 and section.settings.source != 'store' and request.design_mode -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Editor-only hint. Never shown to a shopper: without a block there is
          nothing to render, and a silent empty section is the failure mode that
          made the product page look broken.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <p class="hp-revs__text">
          Add Judge.me’s “Review widget” block to this section to show this
          product’s reviews.
        </p>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Reviews (Judge.me)",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "hp-revs-section",
  "blocks": [{ "type": "@app" }],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Add a Judge.me block below. Reviews always come from the Judge.me app — nothing here is typed in by hand."
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow", "default": "Real Tables · Real Reviews" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "Loved Across Pakistan" },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "text",
      "label": "Intro text",
      "default": "Verified reviews from homes, hosts and restaurants across the country."
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "align",
      "label": "Heading alignment",
      "options": [
        { "value": "left", "label": "Left" },
        { "value": "center", "label": "Centred" }
      ],
      "default": "left"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "source",
      "label": "Reviews to show",
      "info": "Both need a Judge.me block added below — that block is the live widget. This product only: add \"Review widget\", on a product page. All store reviews: add \"All reviews\". With no block, store reviews fall back to Judge.me's stored copy, which displays but is not interactive.",
      "options": [
        { "value": "app", "label": "This product only (Judge.me \"Review widget\" block)" },
        { "value": "store", "label": "All store reviews (Judge.me \"All reviews\" block)" }
      ],
      "default": "app"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "card_style",
      "label": "Card style",
      "info": "Cards carousel dresses Judge.me's Cards Carousel block — the one that leads with the customer's photo — in the theme's design; add that block below. Default keeps Judge.me's own widget exactly as the app renders it — every review reachable, expandable and sortable. Showcase restyles reviews into a drifting rail of fixed-size cards: it looks the part, but it trims each review to a few lines and replaces the app's own controls, so use it as a taster and keep Default wherever people are meant to actually read the reviews.",
      "options": [
        { "value": "default", "label": "Default (Judge.me widget)" },
        { "value": "carousel", "label": "Cards carousel (photo-led)" },
        { "value": "showcase", "label": "Showcase cards" },
        { "value": "list", "label": "Plain list" }
      ],
      "default": "default"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "bg",
      "label": "Background",
      "options": [
        { "value": "cream-soft", "label": "Cream (soft)" },
        { "value": "cream", "label": "Cream" },
        { "value": "paper", "label": "White" },
        { "value": "teal", "label": "Teal" }
      ],
      "default": "cream-soft"
    },
    { "type": "range", "id": "padding_top", "min": 0, "max": 120, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "label": "Top padding", "default": 64 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "padding_bottom", "min": 0, "max": 120, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "label": "Bottom padding", "default": 64 }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Reviews (Judge.me)" }]
}
{% endschema %}
